    ptls_asn1_read_type(bytes, bytes_max, &structure_bit, &type_class, &type_number, decode_error, level, log_ctx);
```

Note that `decode_error` is a pointer passed through. So if an error was set, the recursive call might check it... but actually, `ptls_asn1_read_type` doesn't check `*decode_error` on entry either.

OK I think the key insight is in `ptls_asn1_read_type` itself. Let me look at what happens when the type has a long format (type_number == 31):

```c
if (*type_number == 31) {
    uint32_t long_type = 0;
    const uint32_t type_number_limit = 0x07FFFFFFF;
    int next_byte;
    int end_found = 0;

    while (byte_index < bytes_max && long_type <= type_number_limit) {
        next_byte = bytes[byte_index++];
        long_type <<= 7;
        long_type |= next_byte & 127;
        if ((next_byte & 128) == 0) {
            end_found = 1;
            break;
        }
    }

    if (end_found) {
        *type_number = long_type;
    } else {
        /* This is an error */
        byte_index = ptls_asn1_error_message("Incorrect type coding", bytes_max, byte_index, level, log_ctx);
        *decode_error = PTLS_ERROR_BER_MALFORMED_TYPE;
    }
}
```

If there's an error (not end_found), `byte_index` is set to the return value of `ptls_asn1_error_message`, which returns `byte_index` (its parameter). So `byte_index` remains where it was after the while loop (which could be `bytes_max`).

Then back in `ptls_asn1_validation_recursive`:
```c
size_t byte_index =
    ptls_asn1_read_type(bytes, bytes_max, &structure_bit, &type_class, &type_number, decode_error, level, log_ctx);

if (*decode_error == 0 && log_ctx != NULL) {
    ptls_asn1_print_type(type_class, type_number, level, log_ctx);
}

/* Get the length */
byte_index =
    ptls_asn1_read_length(bytes, bytes_max, byte_index, &length, &indefinite_length, &last_byte, decode_error, level, log_ctx);
```

Note! Even when `*decode_error` is set by `ptls_asn1_read_type`, the code still calls `ptls_asn1_read_length`! It doesn't return early on error.

But `ptls_asn1_read_length` checks `if (byte_index < bytes_max)` before reading, so that should be safe.

Then:
```c
